WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:04.600
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:09.659
Creating applications for mobile Android and
iOS devices is a laborious endeavor which

00:00:09.659 --> 00:00:13.570
most of the time, includes creating a graphical
user interface.

00:00:13.570 --> 00:00:19.030
These are the shiny front-end interfaces that
enable the user to interact with the back-end

00:00:19.030 --> 00:00:20.430
of our applications.

00:00:20.430 --> 00:00:25.470
So what about an algorithm that learns how
to create these graphical user interfaces

00:00:25.470 --> 00:00:28.040
and automates part of this process?

00:00:28.040 --> 00:00:33.260
This piece of work takes one single input
image that we can trivially obtain by making

00:00:33.260 --> 00:00:38.379
a screenshot of the user interface, and it
almost immediately provides us with the code

00:00:38.379 --> 00:00:40.809
that is required to recreate it.

00:00:40.809 --> 00:00:42.510
What an amazing idea!

00:00:42.510 --> 00:00:45.969
The algorithm supports several different target
platforms.

00:00:45.969 --> 00:00:49.620
For instance, it can give us code for iOS
and Android devices.

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:55.100
This code we can hand over to a compiler which
will create an executable application.

00:00:55.100 --> 00:01:00.499
This technique also supports html as well
for creating websites with the desired user

00:01:00.499 --> 00:01:01.499
interface.

00:01:01.499 --> 00:01:06.010
Under the hood, a domain specific language
is being learned, and using this, it is possible

00:01:06.010 --> 00:01:10.100
to have a concise text representation of a
user interface.

00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:14.330
Note that's by no means the only use of domain
specific languages.

00:01:14.330 --> 00:01:19.190
The image of the graphical user interface
is learned by a classical convolutional neural

00:01:19.190 --> 00:01:24.220
network, and this text representation is learned
by a technique machine learning researchers

00:01:24.220 --> 00:01:26.810
like to call Long Short Term Memory.

00:01:26.810 --> 00:01:28.040
LSTM in short.

00:01:28.040 --> 00:01:33.120
This is a neural network variant that is able
to learn sequences of data and is typically

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:38.720
used for language translation, music composition,
or learning all the novels of Shakespeare

00:01:38.720 --> 00:01:40.920
and writing new ones in his style.

00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:45.830
If you were wondering why these examples are
suspiciously specific, we've had an earlier

00:01:45.830 --> 00:01:49.350
episode about this, I've put a link to it
in the video description.

00:01:49.350 --> 00:01:51.750
Make sure to have a look, you are going to
love it.

00:01:51.750 --> 00:01:55.500
Also, this year it will have its twentieth
year anniversary.

00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:57.850
Live long and prosper, little LSTM!

00:01:57.850 --> 00:02:01.650
Now, I already see the forums go up in flames.

00:02:01.650 --> 00:02:07.260
Sweeping generalizations, far-reaching statements
on front end developers around the world getting

00:02:07.260 --> 00:02:08.800
fired and all that.

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:13.800
I'll start out by saying that I highly doubt
that this work would mean the end of front

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:16.010
end development jobs in the industry.

00:02:16.010 --> 00:02:21.050
However, what I do think is that with a few
improvements, it can quickly prove its worth

00:02:21.050 --> 00:02:26.670
by augmenting human labor and cutting down
the costs of implementing graphical user interfaces

00:02:26.670 --> 00:02:27.720
in the future.

00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:32.960
This is another testament to the variety of
tasks modern learning algorithms can take

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:33.960
care of.

00:02:33.960 --> 00:02:38.950
The author also has a GitHub repository with
a few more clarifications, stating that the

00:02:38.950 --> 00:02:42.569
source code of the project and the dataset
will be available soon.

00:02:42.569 --> 00:02:43.830
Tinkerers rejoice!

00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:48.240
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

